4. FIRESTORE STRUCTURE & SECURITY RULES
═══════════════════════════════════════
Collections: users/{uid}; users/{uid}/rooms/{roomId}; roomCodes/{code}; rooms/{roomId}; rooms/{roomId}/members/{uid}; rooms/{roomId}/items/{productId}; rooms/{roomId}/activity/{activityId}.

Write a complete firestore.rules (rules_version = '2') with helper functions isSignedIn(), isMember(roomId) (exists on rooms/{roomId}/members/{request.auth.uid}), isOwner(roomId). Requirements:
- users/{uid}: read/write only by that uid; users/{uid}/rooms/{roomId}: only that uid.
- roomCodes/{code}: signed-in users can get (not list); create only by signed-in user with ownerUid == request.auth.uid and valid fields; update only by the owner (status change).
- rooms/{roomId}: read only members; create by signed-in user where ownerUid == auth.uid; update by members but only limited fields (subtotal, itemCount, memberCount, updatedAt) with sanity checks (non-negative numbers), owner may also update status/name/threshold/deliveryCharge; no delete (or owner only).
- members/{uid}: read by members; create allowed when request.auth.uid == uid (self-join) with role 'member' and room status 'open', OR owner role on room creation by owner; delete by self (non-owner) or owner; no role escalation.
- items/{productId}: read/create/update/delete only by members when room status == 'open'; validate: unitPrice number ≥ 0, totalQty int between 1 and 500, contributions is a map, and the writer may only change their own key in contributions (use request.resource.data.contributions.diff(resource.data.contributions).affectedKeys().hasOnly([request.auth.uid]) for updates; allow full removal when it was only theirs); allow the leave-room cleanup path for the leaving member's own key.
- activity/{id}: read by members; create by members where actorUid == request.auth.uid and createdAt == request.time; no update/delete.
- Default deny everything else. NEVER use "allow read, write: if true".
Also include in README a short "Test the rules" section with Firestore Rules Playground cases (allowed + denied), and a note about the required composite/single-field indexes (activity orderBy createdAt needs none beyond default).

